WEBVTT

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.600
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:45.950
And takes me

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.900
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.050
To the home of wind

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.400
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.640
The stubborn spearsmen still made good!

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.580
Their dark impenetrable wood...

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.260
Anne! Anne!

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.430
What's wrong, Diana?

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.860
I have good news, Anne.
Guess.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.480
Good news?

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.140
Yes. You can have three guesses.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.500
Well...

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.930
Charlotte Gillis is going to be
married in the church after all

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.610
and Mrs. Allan wants us to decorate it!

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.750
No. It's much better than that!

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:07.960
Guess again.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:08.960
Then...

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.250
Jane's mother is going to let
her have a birthday party?

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.240
I can't think what it can be.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.130
Unless it's that Moody Spurgeon saw you
home from prayer meeting last night. Did he?

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.830
I should think not!

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.590
I wouldn't be likely to boast of it
if he did, the horrid creature!

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.390
I knew you couldn't guess it.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.810
We had a letter from Aunt Josephine today,

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.870
and she wants you and me
to go to town next Tuesday

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.890
and stop with her for the Exhibition.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.900
There!

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.720
Oh, Diana, do you really mean it?

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.710
Of course I do.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.330
But I wonder if Marilla will let me go.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.800
She will say that she can't
encourage gadding about.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.830
I'll tell you, we'll get Mother to ask Marilla.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.340
She'll be more likely to let you go then.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.320
I wonder.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.930
It'll be all right.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.060
We'll have the time
of our lives, Anne.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.920
I've never been to an Exhibition,

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.250
and it's so aggravating to hear
the other girls talking about their trips.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.160
Jane and Ruby have been twice,
and they're going this year again.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.900
I'm not going to think about it at all
until I know whether I can go or not.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:24.580
Anne!

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.030
If I did and then was disappointed,

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.990
it would be more than I could bear.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.410
But in case I do go...

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.920
Don't worry, Anne.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.410
I can't even imagine
Marilla allowing me to go.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:47.540
Surprisingly, Marilla agreed to let
Anne go to town and said:

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.860
I would prefer if you didn't go,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:55.620
but if you really want to go,
I'll prepare you a new dress for that day.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:03.770
After all, you've grown up and I don't think
it's a bad idea to go out to see the world.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.080
Marilla!

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.370
Good morning, Diana!

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.930
Good morning, Anne!

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.750
It was a splendid Tuesday morning.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.130
Anne woke up before sunrise,

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:31.630
donned her new clothes and
hastened through Orchard Slope.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.470
Diana and Mr. Barry were waiting for her,
and they were soon on the road.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:12.490
It was a long drive, but Anne and Diana
enjoyed every minute of it.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.550
It was delightful to rattle along everywhere.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:55.060
By noon they arrived at the biggest city
on Prince Edward Island - Charlottetown.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.870
What a big city!

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.160
I only came here once when
I went to Green Gables.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.750
I don't come here often either.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.130
You must be tired, we'll be there soon.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.900
Aunt Josephine
lives in a serene and refined place.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:27.450
It's over there.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.030
It's Aunt Josephine.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.390
Good afternoon, Aunt.
Are you well?

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.790
Not to bad.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.020
So you've come to
see me at last, you Anne-girl.

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.650
Are you both tired?

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.190
Good afternoon, Aunt.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.560
Miss Barry, thank
you very much for your invitation.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.980
How you both have grown!

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.720
And, Anne Shirley,

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.100
you're ever so much
better looking than you used to be.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.550
But I dare say
you know that without being told.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.020
Indeed I didn't,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.660
I know I'm not so freckled as I used to be,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.530
so I've much to be thankful for,

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.550
but I really hadn't dared
to hope there was any other improvement.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.280
I'm so glad you think there is.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:35.820
I'll put the horse in the stable
and rest a little.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.070
I'll have to return soon.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.710
Oh, you're still here?

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:43.390
Hurry up and go rest a little.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.070
Thank you, Mr. Barry.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.040
I'll take you to the sitting room so you can
take off your hats and coats,

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.790
and you will spend the night in
my spare room as promised.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:04.080
They both couldn't help to think about what
happened before in Diana's spare room.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.580
Because of that, Miss Barry had a
chance to come to know and like Anne,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.370
and promised to invite her
to her house in Charlottetown.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.640
Miss Barry's house was
furnished with "great magnificence,"

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.690
and Anne, who had lived in a orphanage,
was rather abashed by the splendor.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.010
And this is the parlor.

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.620
I'll go see about dinner,

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.620
rest here for a while.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.300
Are you impressed?

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.310
It is just like a palace.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:02.530
My!

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.430
Velvet carpet!

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.250
And silk curtains!

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.150
I've dreamed of such things.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:20.090
But I don't believe I feel
very comfortable with them after all.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:24.430
There are so many things in this
room and all so splendid

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:27.160
that there is no scope for imagination.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:32.330
That is one consolation when you are poor.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.280
There are so many more things
you can imagine about.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:40.140
But Anne, wouldn't be nice to live
in a house like this, sometimes?

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.330
Of course! I'm enjoying it!

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:31.780
There's so many people! I never saw
so many people together, before.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:36.100
Of course. People from the whole island
come here to watch the Exhibition.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:38.510
It's nothing like Avonlea.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:42.070
Come, and be careful
to not walk away from me.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:49.970
The Exhibition of Charlottetown
It is held annually in September in Autumn.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.460
A large convention for the islanders.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.620
In the Exhibition grounds there where
agriculture tools, artwork,

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:03.780
and there were contests for farm animals,
and prizes for the best ones.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.950
There were also many other kinds of
activities and contests.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:23.610
Oh, Diana! This one is from Josie Pye!

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:27.180
First Prize, Josie Pye, Avonlea.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.140
Someone you know?

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:31.130
Yes. She's a classmate.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.780
I'm so glad that Josie took first prize!

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.970
This is the first time you praise Josie, Anne.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:39.880
Is that so?

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:51.190
Look! Jane's father took second prize
for Gravenstein apples!

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:53.590
You're right! What a pity.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:01.140
How funny! The superintendent
took first prize for a pig.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.040
Why is that funny?

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.280
Because it's so ridiculous!

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:13.630
From now on I will always remember this
every time I see Mr. Bell praying.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.600
I really like this place.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.780
What a good smell.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.610
I wonder how good the butter
and cheese are this year?

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:27.660
Miss. Barry, the first prize is over there.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:33.430
Oh? These are from Rachel Lynde of Avonlea.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:34.650
Oh! You're right!

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.620
Mrs. Lynde won first place!

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:39.410
Oh my, if it isn't Anne and Diana!

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.340
Oh, Mrs. Lynde!

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:44.420
I never thought of meeting you here!

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:46.960
Congratulations for the first prize, Mrs. Lynde!

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.370
We were invited to the
Exhibition by Miss Josephine Barry.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.890
We're happy to see people from Avonlea win.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.200
Is that so? Thank you.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.710
You must be Miss Josephine Barry?

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:04.900
That's right, Mrs. Lynde,

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:09.180
I hope I can have the honor of
sampling your butter and cheese.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.760
I understand, I shall prepare
it for you as soon as possible.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.520
And where will you go now?

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:18.430
I want to show them the horse races.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:19.500
My!

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.720
If you are interested you could come along.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:24.570
I've already reserved the seats.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:25.510
No.

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.780
I have no such interest.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:33.640
Horse racing is an abomination and
I stay away from that at all costs.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.730
Horse racing is an abomination?

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:42.110
I don't think, though, that I ought
to go very often to horse races, Marilla.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.500
Because they ARE awfully fascinating.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:50.440
Diana got so excited that she offered to bet me
ten cents that the red horse would win.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:54.280
I didn't believe he would,
but I refused to bet.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:58.300
I wanted to tell Mrs. Allan
all about everything,

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.130
and I felt sure it wouldn't do to tell her that.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:06.460
It's as good as an extra conscience to have
a minister's wife for your friend.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.700
And I was very glad I didn't bet,

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:15.030
because the red horse DID win,
and I would have lost ten cents.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.280
So you see that virtue was its own reward.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:24.270
It won! The red horse won!

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:41.920
Oh, Marilla!
It was a never-to-be-forgotten day.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:49.180
We sat on a Merry go round
and saw a man go up in a balloon.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:55.330
I'd love to go up in a balloon, Marilla!
It would be simply thrilling.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:02.180
Miss Barry gave both ten cents each
to have our fortunes told.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:05.400
A little bird picked out our fortune for us.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:10.490
Mine was that I would marry a
dark-complected man who was very wealthy,

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.810
and I would go across water to live.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:17.800
I looked carefully at all the
dark men I saw after that,

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:21.500
but I didn't care much for any of them,

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.930
and anyhow I suppose it's too early
to be looking out for him yet.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:32.660
And Marilla, I was really glad Josie Pye
took first prize for knitted lace.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:36.560
And I was glad that I felt glad,

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:42.220
for it shows I'm improving, if I can rejoice
in Josie's success, don't you think, Marilla?

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:51.840
Miss Barry put us in the spare room,
according to promise.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:59.180
But somehow sleeping in a spare room
isn't what I used to think it was.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:02.220
The things you wanted so much
when you were a child

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:06.770
don't seem half so wonderful
to you when you get them.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:11.250
That's the worst of growing up.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:27.290
Thursday the girls had a drive in the park.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:33.200
The park had massive trees
unseen in Green Gables,

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.330
and many people strolling.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:46.490
And in the evening

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:51.200
Miss Barry took them to a
concert in the Academy of Music.

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:08.680
To Anne the evening
was a glittering vision of delight.

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.690
When Madame Selitsky began to sing,

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:17.050
Anne never thought about anything else,

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.200
and felt the same feeling she has
when she looks up to the stars.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.730
Why are you looking so sad?

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:45.210
Oh! It was such a splendid performance,
so I am so sad that it's all over.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:51.560
I don't see how will I ever
return to common life again.

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:56.720
Well, in that case we should go
to that restaurant

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.870
and see if eating ice cream
will make you feel better.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:03.410
That's great! I'm sure it will
make you feel better, Anne!

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:10.410
That sounded so prosaic,

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:13.030
but to my surprise I found it true.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.830
The ice cream was delicious.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:19.440
I felt much better.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.460
And then Diana said...

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:28.620
I feel so good here.
I believe I was born for city life.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.480
And what is your opinion, Anne-girl?

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:34.740
I think...

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:40.160
I will have to think it over very
seriously before I tell you.

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:46.040
I'll think it over after I go to bed.
That is the best time to think things out.

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:49.660
Is that so? Then think carefully.

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:57.900
Friday brought going-home time,
and Mr. Barry drove in for the girls.

00:18:58.150 --> 00:19:01.060
Well, I hope you've enjoyed yourselves.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:03.320
Indeed we have. It was really fun.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.760
And you, Anne-girl?

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:09.650
I've enjoyed every minute of the time!

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.470
You're a really nice girl.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:21.450
I'm happy that you both had a good time.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.160
Thank you so much, Miss Josephine Barry.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:29.120
And I thought about that question of
yesterday after I went to bed.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.150
And what is your answer?

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:37.270
I wasn't born for city life and I'm glad of it.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:39.540
And then?

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:44.400
It's nice to be eating ice cream at brilliant
restaurants at 11 o'clock at night once in a while,

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:49.570
but as a regular thing I'd
rather be in the east gable at eleven.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:51.360
Sound asleep,

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:55.370
but kind of knowing even in my
sleep that the stars are shining outside

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:58.430
and that the wind is
blowing in the firs across the brook.

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.380
The things you say are really amusing.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:10.950
Thank you, Aunt Josephine!

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:12.370
Take care!

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:17.030
Visit me again some time, you two!

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:18.750
Good bye!

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:29.670
I thought Marilla Cuthbert was an old fool
when I heard she'd adopted

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.800
a girl out of an orphan asylum,

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:34.630
but I guess she
didn't make much of a mistake after all.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:42.810
If I'd a child like Anne in the house all
the time I'd be a better and happier woman.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:36.360
The Avonlea hills.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:38.300
We're almost there.

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:42.640
Oh, but it's good to be going home.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:31.850
Marilla, I'm home!

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:34.220
So you've got back?

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:35.100
Yes!

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.660
And oh, it's so good to be back.

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:43.040
I could
kiss everything, the oven, the clock...

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:50.170
You don't mean to
say you cooked that for me!

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.180
Yes, I did.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:59.190
I thought you'd be hungry after such
a drive and need something real appetizing.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:00.520
Marilla...

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:02.770
Hurry and take off your things,

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:05.430
and we'll have supper
as soon as Matthew comes in.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:06.160
Yes.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.820
I'm glad you've got back, I must say.

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:19.440
It's been fearful
lonesome here without you,

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:23.380
and I never put in four longer days.

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:32.130
After supper Anne sat before the
fire between Matthew and Marilla,

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:34.720
and gave them a full account of her visit.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:38.850
And she concluded happily.

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:44.650
I've had a splendid time, and I feel
that it marks an epoch in my life.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:50.690
But the best of it all was the coming home.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.210
With the help of Miss Stacy,

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:23.590
Anne decides to try for the Queen's Academy.

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:27.170
Next episode of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.130
The Queens Class Is Organized

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:30.250
Please watch!
